{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי שם ה' אקרא", :translit "ki shem hashem ekra", :gloss "for the name of YHWH I call"} {:he "עלי עשב כי שם ה' אקרא", :translit "alei esev ki shem hashem ekra", :gloss "upon grass for the name of YHWH I call"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reading the next word עשב as context: one calls on God's name upon grass, i.e., over each species."}], :frame {:text "The first derivation links Moses' calling on God's name to prayers for rain — the verse is read as the introduction to the song of Ha'azinu, which is associated with the rainy season (Deuteronomy 32:1–2). The second derivation reads the first letters of the words following the verse, connecting 'for the name of YHWH I call' to reciting a blessing over each species, as discussed in Berakhot 35a where Rabbi Levi states that one must not derive benefit from this world without a blessing.", :sources ["Berakhot 35a" "Deuteronomy 32:1-2"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Reading the next word עשב as context: one calls on God's name upon grass, i.e., over each species.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:40:44.095390429Z", :model "glm-5.2"}